WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 146, coming to you on July 30th, 2026. The universe has been busy, let's get into it. Web is tracing how the universe's earliest, mysterious red objects may have simply grown into the galaxies around us today. Little red dots first appeared in web data as compact, distant sources that emerged around 600 million years after the Big Bang, and seem to decline sharply by roughly 1.5 billion

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.960
years after the Big Bang. New analysis indicates these objects did not vanish, but instead developed into the central regions of more familiar galaxies. Researchers combined web infrared observations with Hubble, ultraviolet, and chandra x-ray images of a spiral galaxy already in place 3.3 billion years after the Big Bang. The galaxy's compact central source meets every criterion used to identify little red dots. Yet its proximity is

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:14.400
the same, allows clear views of the surrounding disc and arms. When the same galaxy is artificially placed at higher redshift, the extended structure fades from view, and the object becomes indistinguishable from the distant, little red dot population.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:37.800
This work sits within the ongoing JWST effort to understand early universe galaxy growth that we discussed yesterday. The cosmic spotlight this week focuses on those same web observations of little red dots. The data suggests these compact early objects may simply be the dense cores of galaxies that later grew extended discs and arms.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:54.680
By shifting a nearby spiral galaxy to higher redshift in simulation, researchers showed how its outer structure would vanish from view, leaving only the red central source. The work tightens the link between the mysterious early population and today's mature galaxies.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:04.640
What remains unclear is whether every little red dot follows this evolutionary path or whether some represent a short-lived phenomenon that truly disappears.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:15.340
Now shifting to Boeing's capsule. Boeing's Starliner capsule sits closer to a second crude test flight than many observers anticipated after the initial mission.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:29.440
Engineers continue to assess helium leaks and thruster performance recorded during that flight. A successful uncrewed demonstration could open the door for astronauts to ride the vehicle back to the station before the end of the year.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:40.400
On a different note, a Falcon 9 lifted off from Cape Canaveral Pad 40 carrying the National Reconnaissance Office payload designated USA-619.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:51.840
The mission inserted the signals intelligence satellite into a 63-degree orbit. The first stage touched down at landing zone 2 roughly 8 minutes after liftoff.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:03:04.400
Next up, China completed two launches in one day. A long March 6-A lifted off from Taiyuan carrying the Tengshin Jaishu Shi'en 27-A and 27-B satellites.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:14.560
Earlier the same day, a long March 7-A had sent the Tian Lian 301 data relay satellite from Wenchang into geostationary orbit.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:24.680
Sea surface salinity measurements from the Smoss mission are revealing freshwater and salt patterns across the Pacific that appear before temperature shifts become obvious.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:33.180
These salinity changes could serve as an earlier indicator of developing El Nino conditions than sea surface temperature data alone.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:38.320
A Falcon 9 upper stage will impact the lunar surface on August 5.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:47.480
Scientists intend to examine the resulting crater and ejected material to learn how human-made objects modify the lunar surface environment.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:53.840
The French startup Maya Space has abandoned earlier plans for a suborbital demonstration flight.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.320
Development focus has shifted directly toward an orbital version of its reusable first stage.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:08.610
Students at Johnson Space Center are working on flight hardware, mission planning, and public communication for ongoing human space flight programs.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:14.070
Their projects range from habitat design concepts to real-time operations support.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:21.990
Sophia Space and Caltech received a patent covering modular, large-scale data centers designed to operate in space.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:28.370
The architecture aims to reduce latency and energy demands compared with terrestrial facilities.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:39.310
A new theoretical method combines quantum algorithms with coronagraph techniques to suppress starlight and reveal planets up to 10 billion times fainter than their host stars.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:45.110
The approach targets the specific noise properties that currently limit conventional imaging.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:54.990
If every star were a searchlight, an earth-sized planet would be a firefly hovering just outside the beam, yet the firefly still blocks a few photons.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:05:03.930
Quantum imaging exploits the fact that entangled photon pairs carry correlated information even when one photon never reaches the detector.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:11.030
By measuring subtle interference patterns between the collected starlight and its entangled partner held in the instrument,

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:17.890
astronomers can in principle subtract the star's glare more cleanly than any classical coronagraph allows.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:27.910
The technique requires maintaining entanglement over the long baseline between telescope and spacecraft, a distance measured in tens of thousands of kilometers.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:35.510
Laboratory demonstrations have already shown contrast improvements of several orders of magnitude at visible wavelengths.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:47.150
The open question is whether the same correlation survived the turbulence and thermal noise of a real-space telescope operating for the years needed to survey hundreds of nearby stars.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:57.510
Maintaining face stability across such distances while rejecting stray light from the host star remains an engineering barrier that has not yet been crossed in orbit.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:07.910
Even a modest improvement in contrast would let future missions examine the atmospheres of earth-sized worlds at separations previously considered impossible to resolve.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:16.390
Researchers applied a new stacking algorithm to optical surveys and detected previously invisible fragments in geosynchronous orbit.

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:24.470
The technique aligns multiple short exposures to bring faint objects above the detection threshold without prior orbit knowledge.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:32.130
Orbital radar and thermal data are being combined to estimate regular depth at candidate south polar landing sites.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:38.610
Thicker layers provide better radiation shielding but complicate excavation for habitat foundations.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:46.970
Ground-based radio arrays are now monitoring both active satellites and defunct rocket bodies by detecting their reflected signals.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:52.050
The method works day and night and through cloud cover that blocks optical sensors.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:59.290
NASA has identified South Korea as a primary international contributor to the Artemis lunar base architecture.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:05.790
Joint work will focus on power systems, communications, and resource utilization technology.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:12.750
China's coastal spaceport completed its 50th mission with the recent Tian Lian 301 flight.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:17.050
The site now supports both crude and heavy-lift cargo vehicles.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:24.530
Keep an eye on the next uncrewed starliner test flight, which could clear the way for a crude attempt before the end of the year.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.730
That covers today's space and science news.

